{% extends "doc_page.jinja" %}
{% import "chrome.jinja" as chrome %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "forthcoming_data.jinja" as fc %}
{% block page_title %}Property Testing (Forthcoming) - Netsuke Build System{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    <div class="site-container pt-16 grow flex">

        {{ fc.sidebar('property-testing') }}

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <main id="main-content" class="hm-docs-content flex-1 min-w-0 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-12">

            {% call fc.header('property-testing') %}
                    A fixture proves that one manifest compiles to one build script. A property proves something about every build script a
                    family of manifests can produce: that no action leaks the ambient environment, that declaration order never changes the
                    output, that an unrelated target leaves existing actions alone. RFC 0012 proposes that step for the
                    <a href="{{ fc.href('testing-framework') }}" class="hm-link">Netsukefile testing framework</a>, without adding a scripting language to do it.
            {% endcall %}

            <section id="status" class="mb-16">
                <div class="bg-warning/5 border border-warning/30 rounded-xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="font-bold text-base-content text-sm uppercase tracking-wide mb-3 flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-warning mr-2" data-icon="carbon:information-filled" aria-hidden="true"></span> Status on {{ generated_at.day }} {{ generated_at.strftime('%B %Y') }}
                    </h2>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-accent mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:close" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Not in v{{ netsuke_version }}, and not implemented.</strong> The released CLI has no <code>test</code> command, and nothing on Netsuke's main branch implements <code>forall</code>, <code>mutations</code>, <code>for_all_actions</code>, or <code>result.actions</code>. One seam the design needs, a constructor that pins the recipe shell, already exists for other reasons.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-warning mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:document" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Proposed.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0012-netsukefile-property-testing.md" class="hm-link">RFC 0012</a>, created 2 September 2026, is a proposed extension to <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0007-netsukefile-testing-framework.md" class="hm-link">RFC 0007</a>, which is itself still a proposal. Syntax on this page is the RFC's and may change.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Sequenced after the testing framework.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#10-property-based-testing-of-generated-build-scripts" class="hm-link">Roadmap phase 10</a> depends on the phase 7 result views and case supervisor, none of which has landed. Every phase 10 task is unticked.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:tag" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>No release named.</strong> Neither the RFC nor the roadmap names a target release. This preview describes a design, not a date.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="why" class="mb-16">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:idea" aria-hidden="true"></span> Why properties
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    A Netsukefile is a build-script generator. Its bugs rarely live in the one configuration a fixture pins; they live in the
                    flag ordering nobody tried, the target name with an awkward character, the declaration someone moved. RFC 0012 lets authors
                    state invariants over families of manifests, with the generated build script &mdash; principally its command invocations
                    and environment constructions &mdash; as the subject under test.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">It extends the framework</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Everything here runs inside <code>netsuke test</code> as the <a href="{{ fc.href('testing-framework') }}" class="hm-link">testing framework preview</a> describes it: the same YAML dialect, the same <code>given</code>/<code>when</code>/<code>then</code> steps, the same sandboxed child per case, the same FAIL versus ERROR reporting. Property testing adds a view, a block, a vocabulary, and a lint. Without the framework it has nothing to extend.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Raw Ninja is the wrong subject</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Recipe text depends on the host shell, and per-action environment maps never appear in <code>build.ninja</code> at all. The RFC rejects properties over Ninja text because they end up either flaky across hosts or weakened to the point of vacuity.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Closed on purpose</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">No user-defined predicate functions, strategies, shrinkers, or callbacks. Assertions are MiniJinja expressions the framework already evaluates; generators and mutations come from fixed vocabularies. The RFC rejects a general property-testing DSL because it would reopen the scripting surface RFC 0007 closed.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:information" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>Not the same as Netsuke's own property tests.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#43-determinism-and-manifest-property-testing" class="hm-link">Roadmap &sect;4.3</a>
                        plans Proptest suites that check Netsuke's compiler &mdash; deterministic Ninja emission, expansion invariants, render stability &mdash;
                        inside its Rust test tree. That is Netsuke testing itself. This preview is about authors testing <em>their</em> Netsukefiles.
                        RFC 0012 leaves the internal suites exactly as they are.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="observation" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:view" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span class="min-w-0">Observing actions: <code>result.actions</code></span>
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The framework already proposes three result views: <code>result.manifest</code>, <code>result.graph</code>, and
                    <code>result.ninja</code>. RFC 0012 adds a fourth, available after <code>build_graph</code>, built from the IR with the recipe
                    shell pinned so identical manifests project identically on every host. The view is additive and exposes no internal IR types.
                    Planned under <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#101-structured-observation-of-invocations-and-environments" class="hm-link">roadmap 10.1</a>.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Field</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Meaning (RFC 0012 Table 1)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows text-charcoal-mid">
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">target</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The build edge's primary output target name.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">rule</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The owning rule name.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">argv</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The tokenized command, post-interpolation, pre-quoting.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">env</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The constructed environment map for the invocation.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">cwd</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The working directory, when the action declares one.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">inputs</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The resolved <code>$in</code> bindings.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">outputs</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The resolved <code>$out</code> bindings.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">pool, depfile, dyndep</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">The assigned pool, depfile path template, and dyndep binding, when present.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Tokens, not quoting</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>argv</code> is deliberately the shell-independent token list. Whether a token is quoted correctly for <code>sh</code> or PowerShell is the escaping seam's problem, tested inside Netsuke, not the author's. Helpers mirror the graph view: <code>action(target)</code>, <code>actions_for_rule(name)</code>, and <code>has_action(target)</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">A canonical order</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Actions sort by <code>target</code>, then <code>rule</code>, then the RFC 8785 canonical JSON of the remaining fields &mdash; the canonical value contract of <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0006-ansible-inspired-template-standard-library.md#67-canonical-value-equality" class="hm-link">RFC 0006 &sect;6.7</a>. Declaration order and IR iteration order cannot change the view, so a quantified result reads the same on every run.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mt-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Two quantifiers join the <code>then</code> vocabulary: <code>for_all_actions</code> evaluates each listed expression once per
                    action, binding <code>action</code>; <code>for_all_targets</code> does the same over the graph view, binding <code>target</code>.
                    A failure reports the binding that falsified it with the substituted actual values. Assertions read <code>env</code> in full;
                    every copy that leaves the runner &mdash; failure reports, diagnostics, rendered views, regression files &mdash; carries opaque
                    key tokens and a fixed <code>&lt;redacted&gt;</code> marker instead. Redaction happens only on the way out, so it can never turn
                    a FAIL into an ERROR.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="generation" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:chart-scatter" aria-hidden="true"></span> Bounded generation: the <code class="mx-2">forall</code> block
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    A case may declare <code>forall</code>, a mapping from binding names to domain constructors. The runner expands the domains into a
                    finite family and runs the case's <code>steps</code> once per drawn tuple; drawn values are available wherever <code>let</code>
                    bindings are. The case below draws a target name and a flag ordering, builds the graph, and asserts that no action's environment
                    strays beyond the declared variables and no action lists a source twice.
                    Planned under <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#102-declarative-bounded-generation" class="hm-link">roadmap 10.2</a>.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-8" tabindex="0">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">Proposed syntax, verbatim from RFC 0012 &sect;3</div>
                    {% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
netsuke_test_version: "1.1"

test_command_env_is_closed_over_declared_vars:
  description: Generated invocations never leak ambient environment.
  forall:
    target_name: { path_fragment: { max_len: 12 } }
    flag_order: { permutations_of: ["-O2", "-g", "-Wall"] }
  steps:
    - given:
        let:
          declared_env: []
          name: "{{ target_name }}"
          flags: "{{ flag_order }}"
    - when: build_graph
    - then:
        for_all_actions:
          - "action.env | keys | reject('in', declared_env) | length == 0"
          - "action.inputs | unique | length == action.inputs | length"
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Constructor</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Draws</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Expansion</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows text-charcoal-mid">
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">one_of</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">One member of a finite set</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">Exhaustive</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">permutations_of</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">One ordering of the listed items</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">Exhaustive up to the ceiling</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">int</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">An integer from an inclusive range</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">Exhaustive up to the ceiling</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">path_fragment</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">A sandbox-safe relative path token</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">Sampled</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-6 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-primary whitespace-nowrap">list_of</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">A bounded list over a child domain</td><td class="px-6 py-2 text-sm">Sampled</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    When the Cartesian product of the declared domains fits under the expansion ceiling, every tuple is checked. Above it, the runner
                    samples with a generator seeded with the fixed default seed <code>0</code>, so the default run is deterministic. The ceiling, the
                    sample count, and a per-case deadline are enforced limits. Internally the runner reuses Proptest's generator and regression-file
                    conventions; no Proptest type appears in the dialect.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="mutations" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:compare" aria-hidden="true"></span> Metamorphic mutations
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The most valuable properties of a build-script generator compare two runs rather than inspecting one. These need no predicates
                    from the author, so they ship as a closed <code>mutations</code> vocabulary: the runner derives a mutated manifest, runs the
                    plan-mode pipeline on both, and asserts the named relation. The case below asserts that the generated script ignores
                    declaration order and that adding an unrelated target leaves existing actions alone.
                    Planned under <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#103-metamorphic-relations-coverage-and-adoption" class="hm-link">roadmap 10.3</a>.
                </p>
                <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
                        <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">Proposed syntax, verbatim from RFC 0012 &sect;4</div>
                        {% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
netsuke_test_version: "1.1"

test_generation_is_order_invariant:
  description: Declaration order never changes the generated script.
  subject:
    manifest: fixtures/webapp/Netsukefile
  mutations:
    - permute_declarations
    - insert_irrelevant_target
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] min-w-0" tabindex="0">
                        <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                            <thead class="bg-base-100">
                                <tr>
                                    <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Mutation</th>
                                    <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Relation asserted</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows text-charcoal-mid">
                                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">permute_declarations</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">Actions and Ninja output are identical.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">insert_irrelevant_target</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">Pre-existing actions are unchanged.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">rename_target</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">Actions are isomorphic under the renaming.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">remove_declared_input</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">The declared <code>expect_failure</code> is raised.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    Between them the four cover determinism, order invariance, locality, and rename isomorphism. Mutations compose with
                    <code>forall</code>: each drawn tuple runs the base and the mutated pipeline. Both run inside the framework's killable child
                    process, so a mutant that diverges or never terminates cannot wedge the runner.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="coverage" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:replicate" aria-hidden="true"></span> Coverage and replay
                </h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Shrink, persist, replay</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">On failure the runner delta-reduces the drawn tuple towards domain minima, reports the smallest falsifying binding set it finds, and names both the seed and that tuple. It then persists seed and tuple in a regression file under the test tree, and later runs replay it before generating anything fresh. <code>--seed &lt;n&gt;</code> selects a reported seed, but a seed alone is not a replay: without the persisted tuple inputs the run samples afresh from that seed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">The coverage completeness lint</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">When a rule interpolates a command or constructs an environment and no test in the suite covers one of its actions with a quantified or metamorphic assertion, the run names the rule. The report is advisory by default; <code>--strict-coverage</code> makes it a failure. New invocation shapes opt into the property contract deliberately instead of drifting past it. The lint arrives last in the phase.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">Adapted: two flags named in RFC 0012 prose, which shows no combined invocation</div>
                    {% highlight 'netsuke-console' %}
$ netsuke test --seed <n>
$ netsuke test --strict-coverage
{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    Everything else on the command line &mdash; filters, <code>--tag</code>, <code>--timeout</code>, <code>--json</code>, the exit
                    codes &mdash; is the framework's, described under <a href="{{ fc.href('testing-framework', 'command') }}" class="hm-link">the command</a>
                    in its preview. The RFC does not specify how the seed, tuple, or falsifying binding appear in JSON output, and does not show a
                    rendered property failure, so neither is sketched here.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="shallow-end" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> From the shallow end
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Property testing is opt-in by dialect version. Existing <code>netsuke_test_version: "1.0"</code> files parse and run unchanged;
                    only a file that uses <code>forall</code>, <code>mutations</code>, or <code>result.actions</code> declares <code>"1.1"</code>.
                    The shallow end is therefore the framework's own quick start: one file beside the manifest, one case, plain <code>netsuke test</code>.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-8" tabindex="0">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">netsuke-tests/hello.yml &mdash; dialect 1.0, verbatim from the testing framework UX design &sect;14.1</div>
                    {% highlight 'netsuke' %}
netsuke_test_version: "1.0"

test_hello_target_is_generated:
  steps:
    - when: generate_ninja
      then:
        - result.ok
        - result.graph.has_target("build/hello.txt")
{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <ol class="space-y-4 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">1. A 1.0 test file</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Fixed fixtures and assertions over the manifest, graph, and Ninja views. No properties, no version bump. See the <a href="{{ fc.href('testing-framework', 'shallow-end') }}" class="hm-link">testing framework's shallow end</a>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">2. <code>mutations</code>, with no expressions</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Declare <code>"1.1"</code>, point a case at an existing manifest, and list <code>permute_declarations</code>. The relation is built in, so the first property needs no predicate at all &mdash; the RFC's example is five lines of intent.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">3. <code>forall</code> over small finite sets</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>one_of</code>, <code>permutations_of</code>, and small <code>int</code> ranges enumerate exhaustively under the ceiling, so every combination is checked rather than sampled, with <code>for_all_actions</code> assertions over each. Sampled domains such as <code>path_fragment</code> and <code>list_of</code> come after, with seeds and replay.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">4. Read the coverage lint</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The advisory report names each rule whose command or environment no property covers. It is the signpost to the deep end: a list of the next properties worth writing.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">5. Gate on <code>--strict-coverage</code></h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Once the list is empty, make it stay empty: an uncovered rule fails the run, so a new invocation shape cannot land without a property.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    No tutorial exists yet. Roadmap 10.3.4 plans the users' guide testing chapter for the property dialect, after the dialect has been
                    dogfooded on Netsuke's own example manifests.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="confidence" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:certificate-check" aria-hidden="true"></span> Confidence
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Every guarantee below is designed, not shipped. The phase's own success criterion is that the RFC's worked examples run green
                    deterministically on a machine with no compiler and no network, and that a seeded failure replays identically from its reported
                    seed and tuple inputs.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Plan mode only</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Properties observe plan-mode projections and never execute the build: no network, no ambient environment, no build execution. Fuzzing Ninja, the shell, or spawned processes is an explicit non-goal.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Deterministic by default</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">No ambient clock, the fixed default seed <code>0</code>, and every report naming the seed in effect. Roadmap 10.2.2 requires repeated default runs to be byte-identical; the pinned shell makes <code>result.actions</code> identical across hosts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Bounded and killable</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The ceiling, sample count, and per-case deadline are enforced limits, and each case runs in a child the runner can kill. Unbounded generation and coverage-guided exploration are non-goals.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Proven to catch something</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Roadmap 10.3.1 accepts each mutation only once a seeded suite shows it detecting a deliberately introduced generator defect. And a 1.0 runner fails closed on a 1.1 file with its unsupported-version diagnostic rather than misparsing it.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-warning mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:help" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <div class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <p class="mb-2"><strong>Open questions in <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0012-netsukefile-property-testing.md#open-questions" class="hm-link">the RFC</a>.</strong></p>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                            <li>The default expansion ceiling and sample count. The RFC suggests something between 16 and 24, set by measuring realistic suites; roadmap 10.3.3 records the measurement.</li>
                            <li>Whether <code>result.actions</code> should land early, inside the framework's phase 7 result-view work, so example-based tests can use it before properties arrive. The roadmap currently keeps it in phase 10.</li>
                            <li>Whether delta reduction over the drawn tuple is enough, or whether sampled list domains need element-wise shrinking. Limited shrinking is a stated non-goal, not an oversight.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="access" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:accessibility" aria-hidden="true"></span> Accessibility &amp; localization
                </h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Inherited output modes</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Property failures are <code>netsuke test</code> reports, so they follow its display policy flags &mdash; <code>--color</code>, <code>--emoji</code>, <code>--progress</code>, <code>--accessibility</code> &mdash; and its stream-purity contract: one JSON document per completed run under <code>--json</code>. See the <a href="{{ fc.href('testing-framework', 'access') }}" class="hm-link">framework's accessibility notes</a> and <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/" class="hm-link">the CLI reference</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Localized like the rest</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The framework design localizes every human-facing string like the rest of the CLI, and roadmap 7.6.2 plans Fluent keys for report lines, diagnostics, and warnings. Property reports are no exception: every human-readable message Netsuke emits is localized by default, so a falsified property, a replay hint, or a coverage warning reads in the user&rsquo;s locale, while seeds, rule names, and drawn values stay exactly as generated.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Redacted by design</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Environment keys and values never leave the runner in clear: reports, diagnostics, and persisted regression files carry opaque key tokens and <code>&lt;redacted&gt;</code>, following ADR-009's redact-at-the-boundary rule.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    The RFC itself puts a plain-language summary before each YAML example for screen readers, which this page follows. It is silent on
                    how a falsifying binding or a minimized tuple should read under <code>--accessibility on</code>; roadmap 7.6.3 plans to document
                    how the framework's results and diagnostics render in that mode, and property reports would need the same treatment.
                </p>
            </section>

{{ fc.footer('property-testing') }}

        </main>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
